WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:03.461
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.117
- July 17th,

00:00:09.117 --> 00:00:10.228
2007.

00:00:10.228 --> 00:00:13.590
(bright upbeat music continues)

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.493
Do you remember where you were that day?

00:00:16.493 --> 00:00:17.440
What you were feeling?

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.273
What you were doing?

00:00:18.273 --> 00:00:19.620
What you're up to that month?

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.380
I was in a little town in Wales taking

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.280
in these castle ruins, it's beautiful.

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:27.820
That experience left a mark on my brain

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:28.823
for sure.

00:00:28.823 --> 00:00:31.170
(camera shutters)

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.140
What about December 13th, 2014?

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:37.790
What were you up to?

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.450
That was a less memorable
experience for me,

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.630
but it was just a normal
day at the house, Iz

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.830
was reading a book, Henry
had a fever, he was sick.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.550
We had put up Christmas decorations

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.690
and gone on a walk on the little trail

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:50.870
in front of our house.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.263
Not a super memorable day.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:54.960
Somewhere in your brain,

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.810
there is stored a memory of what happened

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.040
on those two days for you.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.070
But it's probably impossible to dig it up.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:03.070
You probably have no
idea what you were doing

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.590
on July 17th, 2007.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:07.900
Remembering this stuff is hard

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.740
because our brains aren't built for it.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:10.940
Think of this circle

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.610
as what you're able to
recall and actively think

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.450
about at any given moment.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.860
You've got a few memories
and pieces of information

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.410
that are really close by
in case you need them.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.130
Like when I asked you what your
phone number is, you've got

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.050
that super close, nice and
bright, easy to retrieve.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.040
But when I ask you to remember the name

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.210
of your favorite school teacher,

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.440
you have to stretch a little bit,

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.300
but you're eventually able to retrieve it,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.750
throw it into this little
circle and remember it.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.980
If I ask you to tell me when
was the last time you saw

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.100
a hamster, you'll scan
these bright memories

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.160
and you won't find it.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.460
The last time you saw a hamster

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.380
is probably way out here
lurking in some dark part

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.510
of your brain.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.730
It's there, but it's totally unretrievable

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.690
and what's scary, is you don't even know

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.100
that you can't remember it.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.160
You can't remember that
you can't remember it.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.083
You saw a hamster at some point,

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:02.883
but it's gone.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.380
The vast majority of your life,

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.680
the thousands of days that
you've spent on this Earth

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.070
are all out here with a few bright spots

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.350
for really important memories

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.770
that you can retrieve pretty easily,

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.720
like the first time you left the country,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.330
or the day you met your significant

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:19.690
other, or that time you broke your wrist.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.880
But most of this stuff
is totally unretrievable,

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.540
which is totally fine.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:24.730
You probably don't care to remember

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.020
that time you bumped your knee

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.820
on the door of your
friend's apartment in 2015.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.040
But what about that gathering

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.520
of friends that you had that same month,

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.670
where the conversation was
flowing and the food was so good

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.053
and you felt alive and excited about life?

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.160
To me, that's worth remembering.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.200
What was the best experience
you had in April of 2011?

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.260
What made you happy that month?

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.540
You have no idea.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.710
And maybe you don't care
to know what happened

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.410
in April of 2011,

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:55.993
but I do.

00:02:55.993 --> 00:02:58.710
(bright upbeat music)

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.000
I think it's travesty

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.530
that so much of our life
is simply forgotten.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:05.700
I want my memory to look more like this,

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.070
with the bright spots, not
just being the memorable trips

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.860
I went on, but also the
mundane experiences,

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.880
the things that happened
to me years and years ago.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.800
What if I could somehow
remember simultaneously

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.740
the memorable stuff?

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:19.840
Like the first time I wandered

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.960
through Rio de Janeiro?
(camera shutters)

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.040
And the mundane, but special
stuff, like that time

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.180
we went to the park with Iz's parents

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.610
when Henry was two years old, back in 2015

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.800
and had like a really amazing day.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.100
I had great conversation,
Henry was in a good mood,

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.020
everyone was happy.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:37.560
That was a special day.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.940
And I wanna remember it.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.710
I've been thinking about this
predicament for many years

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.740
and developing processes, and workflows

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.260
and ways that I can use photos and video

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.355
to create a world, where I
can easily retrieve memories.

00:03:50.355 --> 00:03:53.105
(camera humming)

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.470
I wanna share with you some
of the guiding principles

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.210
that I use, the workflows, the softwares,

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:04.430
the philosophies that I use to do this.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:07.010
And hopefully, if you're
interested, you can start

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.973
to construct a similar world of memories.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.840
Solving this issue has to do with this.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.200
You've got this amazing camera

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.330
with you at all times, but
you've probably noticed

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:22.010
that the solution isn't just
to take a bunch of photos.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:24.110
The familiar situation is you take a bunch

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.300
of photos of things that
you probably wanna remember

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.707
and then you have so many photos
just sitting on your phone

00:04:28.707 --> 00:04:30.560
and you don't really know
what to do with them.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.150
You never end up actually looking at them.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.610
It's the great paradox
of digital photography

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:37.050
and it's really easy
to get out of control.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.570
My approach to this involves photos,

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.570
but it does not involve just taking photos

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:42.960
of everything you want to remember.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.820
Secondly, I have to say that my approach

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.010
to this is influenced by the research

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.180
that's been done on what
happens to your brain

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:50.820
when you take a picture of something.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.580
If you're walking along the cliff sides

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.870
of Lima, Peru, and you
pull out your camera

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:58.020
to take a photo, something
happens in your brain.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.720
Your brain starts recording less

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.010
of the non-visual sensations around you.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.840
So that bird that's hanging

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.610
out next to you, or the sound
of the ocean, or the traffic,

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:10.010
or the breeze, the smell,
the way you're feeling.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:13.330
In short, the more immersive
experience of being in a place.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.740
Instead, your brain hones
in on the visual aspects

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:17.573
of the scene.

00:05:17.573 --> 00:05:18.890
So, a year later, if someone asks

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.580
you what Lima, Peru is
like, you will probably

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:25.140
have a better visual memory
of your experience there,

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.810
but you won't remember
what you were feeling,

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.020
you won't remember the general experience.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.570
You'll remember the thing
that you took a photo of.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.180
Okay, you're probably done hearing me talk

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.070
about the theory

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:37.680
behind how I do this and the
broad research and all that.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.070
Now let me show you
how I actually do this.

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:43.910
The key to remembering your
life is deleting photos.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:44.743
Okay?

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:45.645
I'm gonna say that again.

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:49.630
The key to remembering your
life is deleting photos.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:51.750
The antithesis to remembering your life

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.060
is too many photos.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:56.060
You take a lot of photos,
many of them, you don't want.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.050
Having them in your world

00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.380
without you actually wanting

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.840
them will cause more stress than joy.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.660
Get used to deleting photos often.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.940
I haven't reviewed all of
the many, many softwares

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:08.810
that exist out there for
doing this sort of thing.

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:11.110
The one thing I can say is
that you want a software

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.240
that when you take a
photo with your phone,

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.610
it immediately uploads it to the internet

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.100
and downloads it to your computer.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.640
So that any photo you add on your computer

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.230
or you take with your phone,
you see them in both places.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.540
I know Google Photos
does this, Apple Photos

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.130
does this, Lightroom Mobile does this.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.080
I'm sure it's becoming

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.850
more and more of just a standard feature

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.470
for all photo softwares, so
it shouldn't be hard to find.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:35.630
The reason you want to do this,

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:37.920
is because the first step
to actually getting a handle

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.900
on your pictures, is having
them in one central library.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.230
And that's why I very strongly
recommend using software,

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:45.590
that when you take a
picture on your phone,

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:46.730
it shows up on your computer.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.880
Instead of scrolling through
Instagram or Facebook

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:50.440
for once during the day, scroll

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.640
through your own pictures and delete them.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.650
In this process of deleting
photos and making that a habit,

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.480
you get the first benefit of this system,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.970
which is, you will be looking

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:01.283
at your photos more often.

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:09.860
You get to a new place, or you
have a fun weekend somewhere

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.660
and you take a bunch of
photos and videos of something

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:16.557
and you're like, "I don't
really need that video.

00:07:16.557 --> 00:07:19.097
"That was a cool video
and it was a cool thing,

00:07:19.097 --> 00:07:23.207
"but I don't necessarily need
to remember that in 15 years,

00:07:23.207 --> 00:07:25.214
"so get rid of it."

00:07:25.214 --> 00:07:28.950
(bright upbeat music begins)

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:30.800
Like this.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.967
I took this photo in
Montreal, because I was like,

00:07:32.967 --> 00:07:35.267
"Oh, I wanna remember
the Lonely Planet guide

00:07:35.267 --> 00:07:38.640
"that I was using and the
way the trees looked."

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:42.680
No, and I didn't take one, I took a bunch.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.650
Let's get rid of literally all of these.

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.550
Interviewing Hillary Clinton,

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:49.650
yes, definitely I want that photo.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:52.010
That was a really cool day,
I wanna remember that day.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:53.850
I'll pick one and delete the others.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.360
I don't need 10 pictures
of Henry and Oliver

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.680
at the pediatrician.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:58.960
Okay, so I'll go through.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:07:59.950
That's a cute shot.

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:02.490
That's not, and I probably have 15.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:03.710
That's not.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:05.860
You relive the memories
as you're deleting.

00:08:07.295 --> 00:08:08.128
Okay.

00:08:08.128 --> 00:08:09.600
As I'm deleting them here, it's deleting

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:10.710
them on my phone too,

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.370
so the clutter's just producing
across both my devices.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.290
Okay, so from that pediatrician day,

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.510
I've got four photos.

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:21.180
Are you starting to see how this works?

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.530
Actually, I've started
to enjoy this process.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:24.363
It seems like work,

00:08:24.363 --> 00:08:25.790
but it's actually an effort that helps

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:27.340
you relive these memories

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.410
and reduce clutter at the same time.

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:30.910
If that photo doesn't do something

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.251
to your brain, chemistry, get rid of it.

00:08:33.251 --> 00:08:34.740
If you're a photographer,

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.200
I'm gonna talk to you for a second.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.070
If you're not a photographer,
go get a drink of water,

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:41.570
because I'm about to
use a bunch of jargon.

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:42.910
All right, photographer time.

00:08:42.910 --> 00:08:44.097
Photographers are probably thinking,

00:08:44.097 --> 00:08:45.687
"I need to edit my photos,

00:08:45.687 --> 00:08:47.457
"I want to work with RAW photos."

00:08:47.457 --> 00:08:50.290
Lightroom was the promise for us,

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:52.327
where they'd say, "No, you can have all

00:08:52.327 --> 00:08:54.300
"of your photos organized and work

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.347
with them in RAWs, edit
them in a robust software,

00:08:57.347 --> 00:08:59.640
"but also see them on your phone."

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:00.880
It seemed like the promise,

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:02.210
and for the past six years,

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:04.560
I've been using Lightroom
both to edit my photos

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.720
and to organize them and catalog them.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.620
But as my photo library grew,

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.650
it started to get out of control.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:14.250
Trying to manage RAW photos
that are 40 to 50 megabytes

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.270
each, it's just insanity

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:18.250
when you have tens of thousands of photos.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:21.040
Theoretically, Lightroom
is the perfect software.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:23.890
But in practice, as the catalog grows,

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:25.600
it starts to become less stable

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.090
and difficult to work with.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:30.270
I recently had a catalog
fail and all of that metadata

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:33.190
that was embedded into
the catalog was lost.

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:35.715
The edits, the geo data, the keywords.

00:09:35.715 --> 00:09:40.715
And that made me realize that
I can't rely on a program,

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.240
a catalog file,

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.650
to have all of that really
important information.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.880
So I still use Lightroom
to edit my photos,

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:49.790
but then I immediately
export them as high-res JPEGs

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.270
with all of the metadata embedded

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.240
into it, including the
geotag and everything.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:56.760
And then I imported into
Apple Photos as baked.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.410
I call it baked, because I like that idea

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.890
of baking the RAW into a nice JPEG,

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:02.250
that's like three megabytes.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.360
They share much quicker, you
don't have to export anything.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:06.710
I love Lightroom and I wish

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:09.780
that it could just be the
perfect cure-all software,

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:10.613
but it's not.

00:10:10.613 --> 00:10:13.060
All right, I'm done talking
to just photographers.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:14.170
Let's get back into this.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:16.560
Just to remember the mantra
so far, the key to remembering

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.050
your life is deleting photos.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:20.460
Okay, so we've talked about the technology

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:21.870
and kind of the workflow.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:23.740
What about when you're
in a beautiful place?

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:25.960
What if you're in Porto,
Portugal, which is one

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.280
of my favorite cities
right now, by the way,

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:29.310
and it's sunset,

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:31.850
and you're in this beautiful
place, and this view,

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.900
and you want to document
it and remember the night

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:36.720
and the buzz in the air.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.500
How do you document that place

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:39.720
and that memory in a way

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.330
that will help you relive
it later on in your life?

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.020
Back to that research that we talked

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:45.530
about, when you're taking
a picture of something,

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:47.540
you're making a little bit of a trade off.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.200
You're enhancing your visual memory,

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:52.150
but you're sacrificing some
of the other sensory memories

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.350
of the smell, and the vibe
and the emotional experience

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:56.183
of being there.
(soft upbeat music)

00:10:56.183 --> 00:10:58.500
There is local music being played,

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.696
there's a smell of summer in the air.

00:11:00.696 --> 00:11:02.410
There is just a vibe

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:04.400
of excitement and relaxation

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.719
that you can't capture with a camera.

00:11:06.719 --> 00:11:09.719
(soft upbeat music)

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:13.480
It's amazing light,

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.910
I just wanna take a bunch of pictures.

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:18.450
And so, that's what we do at first.

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:19.850
We take a bunch of pictures.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.200
But then comes the most important part.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.720
After you've taken a bunch of pictures,

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:28.660
gotten it out of your
system, put your camera away

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:31.040
and let your brain do the documenting.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:34.873
(soft upbeat music continues)

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:40.230
You have that visual
information saved in photos.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:42.200
Now it's time for your brain to capture

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:44.490
the other sensory information,

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:47.060
the smell, the breeze, the sounds,

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:49.340
all of that experience that
you can't get with a camera.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.006
The photos you took will
be put under a computer.

00:11:52.006 --> 00:11:54.390
I'll delete probably 80 or 90%

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:57.150
of 'em, keep just 17
pictures from that night

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.670
of the hundreds I took and
those photographs will serve

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:02.800
as a link, a gateway to that memory.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:03.970
And it will hopefully spark

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:06.850
in my brain, all of that
other sensory information

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:08.403
that I experienced that night.

00:12:08.403 --> 00:12:11.920
(camera shuttering)

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:15.980
Okay, so now, it's time for
what I say is the payoff

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:16.813
of all of this.

00:12:16.813 --> 00:12:19.610
If you are intentional in your efforts

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:22.900
to cool down and delete photos,

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:26.060
and to be in moments
when you're in moments,

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:28.010
and to let those sink into your brain

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:29.120
and to capture them as well,

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:31.450
if you do all of that intentional labor,

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:33.890
now you are in the amazing
position to be able

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:36.768
to what I call, explore your memory.

00:12:36.768 --> 00:12:39.935
(bright upbeat music)

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:46.700
To go back and to spark those moments

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:48.270
and those feelings again.

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.230
So, you can do this several ways,

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:51.430
you can do this a million ways.

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:54.350
But the way I choose to do it
is to go into Apple Photos,

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:56.850
where I have all of my
photos in one catalog

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:58.020
and to zoom out.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:01.520
You can zoom out to years,

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:03.690
which is basically, you
can see an entire map

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:07.010
of all of your photos year by year.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:07.843
Pick a year.

00:13:07.843 --> 00:13:12.843
2015, we went driving in Florida
to look at some manatees.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:15.960
Again, I'd totally forgotten
about this experience

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:17.240
until right now.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.670
Here we are, we go in, saw the manatees

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:20.890
and then we drove back.

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:23.940
And I remember our car broke down.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:25.312
We were on the side of the road

00:13:25.312 --> 00:13:29.213
for a bunch of hours
waiting for a tow truck.

00:13:29.213 --> 00:13:31.260
And there we are.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:33.840
And I took a bunch of photos that night,

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.630
just because I had nothing
to do and the light

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:36.530
was pretty cool.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:38.010
I probably had a couple 100 photos,

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:39.480
just because I was bored,

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:44.480
but I only kept four
photos of the breakdown.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.490
And that's enough for me.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:50.140
That image right there of the
van broken down on the side

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:54.360
of the road is enough to
spark that entire memory,

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.020
because it's so vivid and it captures

00:13:57.020 --> 00:13:58.250
so much of that moment.

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:01.880
That shot mixed with Iz
sitting there reading

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:04.190
a book by the light of her phone

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:06.650
in her swimsuit is enough to spark

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:09.410
that memory for me, and to help me relive

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:11.563
it, and to feel all those feelings again.

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:17.200
So that is effectively
the general approach

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.660
to how I think about documenting my life.

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:23.130
It maybe seem simple, take
photos and organize them.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:25.520
But there's some foundational concepts

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.080
in there that I think people miss

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:29.120
out on that make a huge difference.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:31.950
So to review, find a good photo software

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:33.220
that syncs your photos

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:35.090
to the internet and to your devices,

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.563
so you have a backup and you
have them easily accessible

00:14:37.563 --> 00:14:39.030
on both devices.

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:39.920
This will lead you

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:42.560
to deleting your photos,
deleting, deleting.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:44.870
Delete most of the photos you take.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:47.010
Only keep the ones that spark a memory

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:48.590
or spark joy for you.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:50.570
When you go on trips or you're in places

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:52.660
that you really want to remember,

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:54.080
have some camera free time.

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:56.580
Put the camera away and try to document

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:59.450
the experience with your brain only.

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:02.180
This, paired with
intentional photographing

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:06.117
of that place, will later lead
to more immersive memories

00:15:06.117 --> 00:15:09.070
and a better way to
relive those experiences

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:12.140
in a way that isn't just visual,
but is sensory generally.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:13.660
And finally, the payoff.

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:14.493
Don't forget

00:15:14.493 --> 00:15:16.190
if you do all those other steps

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:18.880
to then explore your
memories, spend time going

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:20.400
through those old photos

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:22.800
and remembering why you kept them,

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:25.880
trying to relive those experiences
that you want to relive.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:27.870
And this also helps you
come to terms with the fact

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:29.250
that everything is temporary

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:31.115
and it passes away, and changes,

00:15:31.115 --> 00:15:33.950
and that things that seem
like a really big deal,

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:37.310
that are stressful now,
probably won't be stressful

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:39.200
in 2 1/2 years when you're scrolling

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.018
through the photos from today.

00:15:41.018 --> 00:15:44.185
(bright upbeat music)

